WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.296
Renting a GPU provides temporary access to accelerator hardware without purchasing, installing, powering, and maintaining it.

00:00:07.296 --> 00:00:16.259
A user can provision a remote machine, run a model or data job, collect the results, and release the resource.

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:24.805
This is valuable when the requirement is short-lived, uncertain, or beyond the memory and performance of available local systems.

00:00:24.805 --> 00:00:38.354
Common reasons include evaluating a larger model before buying hardware, completing a temporary fine-tuning or batch-processing job, absorbing a demand spike, and giving a distributed team access to a consistent environment.

00:00:38.354 --> 00:00:43.148
Rental also converts a large capital purchase into operational spending.

00:00:43.148 --> 00:00:53.362
That flexibility is useful when utilization would be too low to justify ownership or when the required GPU type may change between projects.

00:00:53.362 --> 00:00:56.489
The trade is not free convenience.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:07.954
The renter gives up some physical control and accepts provider capacity limits, network dependence, ongoing metered charges, and responsibility for cloud identity, access, and data handling.

00:01:07.954 --> 00:01:10.247
Setup time is billable.

00:01:10.247 --> 00:01:15.458
An idle instance can cost as much as a productive one.

00:01:15.458 --> 00:01:24.004
Uploading sensitive prompts or documents changes the data boundary, and persistent disks or snapshots can outlive the compute session.

00:01:24.004 --> 00:01:31.300
Begin with a defined job: model, runtime, memory need, expected duration, data classification, and acceptable interruption.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:36.511
Compare the complete rental cost with local capacity and managed alternatives.

00:01:36.511 --> 00:01:46.308
Renting succeeds when it delivers the needed compute for a controlled period and the environment is shut down or deleted deliberately afterward.

00:01:46.308 --> 00:01:53.187
It fails when provisioning is easy enough that ownership, cost, and residual data become invisible.
